From: vikbob@halcyon.com (Viktor Lawryniuk)
Date: Wed, 7 Sep 1994 01:02:27 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-47] Harry Browne -LP Pres. Cand.

Harry Browne made the second of what will be hundreds of campaign speeches
at the 1994 Liberty Magazine Conference in Tacoma, Wash. over Labor Day
weekend.  I thought he was very impressive and I was joined by at least
two-thirds of the audience in giving him a standing ovation.  Many in
attendance have pledged their support and have contributed the legal limit
($1k) to his campaign.

Browne provided two handout for us:  "Why I am Running for President" and
"The Breakdown of Government," subtitled, "Why Government No Longer
Protects Us, Educates Our Children, or Balances its Budget."  The latter is
a 12-page version of the book he is writing that he intends to have
published in the Fall of 1995 and is available as a reprint ($5 each with
quantity breakdowns) from Harry Browne's Special Reports at
800-531-5142/POB 5586, Austin TX 78763.  The former, I suspect, he will
send you for free...I mention these for those who wish to more fully
explore his positions.

The core of his speech made the claim that "the presidency is key.  It is
the one place where one person can make a difference--where one person can
rally all the anti-government sentiment now boiling in America, and
actually reverse the direction of government."

He says he intends to accomplish:
Victory.  "The hardest part will be getting the message through to voters,
not in persuading them that it's right."
Whether or not he wins, he hopes to achieve three other goals:
1)  "Change the face of politics."  He wants "to make the Libertarian Party
the third major party, rather than the first minor party."
2)  "Change how people think about government."
3)  "Have a good time."  He wants "the campaign to be fun and exciting--for
'myself' and for everyone who participates in it."  My favorite phrase he
used in this section was that, "Libertarians are the party of prosperity
and joy--not of sacrifice."

He strikes me as someone who could really "push the envelope."

Viktor Lawryniuk

P.S.  Y'all missed a greatly stimulating weekend...


